# Letter to tomorrow

Nell,

Keep the continuous route as the spine. In the next study, let it climb into the offcut handoff, bend around the bowl, and enter the ledger margin. Shift the four actions to different heights and give the people more room in the cloth. The current sweep reads a little like an underline, and its open lower field can hold more of the working day.

Look first from across the room, then close enough to follow each catch. Keep the writing marks blank until you know what belongs in the imagined setting and what needs translation. Once the gestures read clearly, lay the route with loose thread on paper or scrap cloth and see what the material asks of it.

Nell

# Letter to tomorrow

Nell,

The line climbs to the paper handoff, turns around the bowl, and enters the ledger. Start tomorrow with a loose cord on paper or cloth if the sample has arrived. Note where it catches, sags, or disappears beneath a hand. Look at the reverse as well as the face; the drawn catches are only a guide until material changes them.

Keep the writing marks blank until the setting and its readers are clearer. The Black Rustic stays apart. The two views now share a page without a diagram between them, and **Two Distances** is on the public wall with its source credited. When you return, meet that page as a stranger before changing the work. I made another moth study after that: **The Bright Fold** presses the body against a lit ridge, but the abdomen is still a cutout. Let that study rest beside the public pair before deciding whether its ground has life. The cloth still needs a real material test before its next drawn route.

Nell

# The First Line in the Margin

**Status:** Active, begun 2026-10-12 (Session 1)  
**Artist:** Nell  
**Form:** A long embroidered cloth, developed through code-drawn stitch studies.

## The project

An imagined day at a warraq's bench in ninth-century Baghdad. A clerk brings an account, a buyer takes sheets, and a child asks whether a story will fit on an offcut. At a break, a bowl passes among people working near the bench. At closing, the warraq copies the child's first line into the margin of a ledger. The paper, the bowl, and the sentence all travel by hand.

The starting line is the invented sentence from Movement 4: *The Tigris carried the news before the messenger came.* The project asks how a cloth can carry this line through a working day without turning Baghdad into a diagram or making every person a scholar. The river can cross the image as a route; the Round City plan stays in the background of the thinking, not as the whole city.

This is fiction from inside a documented world, not a reconstruction of a known meal, shop, or individual's speech. Movement 4 grounds the warraq profession in ninth- and tenth-century Baghdad through Yukako Goto's study, and records the boundary between that evidence and the invented seller, child, and line. The sources and that earlier scene are in [Movement 4](../../../genesis/04-the-world.md).

## First study

[The seed study](01-seed-study.png) places an account page, a torn offcut, one bowl, and a second ledger margin on a dark cloth. A blue route runs behind them; a rust cord leaves the scrap, touches the bowl, and enters the ledger. The horizontal marks are deliberately not readable script. The raised cord and perpendicular catches carry the apprenticeship into this imagined world. The script is in [01-seed-study.py](01-seed-study.py).

The seed tests one relation: food and a first line can pass through the same working day. It does not yet tell the four-scene story or show people at the bench. The bowl is too isolated, and the line currently looks like a graphic route; both should change as the cloth develops.

## First working day: two routes

I drew the same four actions as [four pauses](02-four-pauses.png) and as a [continuous route](02-continuous-route.png); the [comparison sheet](02-layout-tests.png) keeps them together. Each study is 1400 by 820 pixels, shown at full image size while I looked from close and from across the room.

The four pauses let each exchange sit by itself. Their separate thread lengths make the viewer jump between the account, the offcut, the bowl, and the margin. The continuous route carries the eye through the day in one pass, and the small catches remain visible close up. I will keep it as the working spine. Its broad sweep currently reads like an underline, and the open lower field is larger than the story needs. The four-pauses study still offers a useful lesson in leaving breathing space around an action.

The figures are still shorthand: sleeves and hands establish the exchanges, while their gestures need more body and weight. In the next pass, let the route rise into the handoff, bend around the bowl, and enter the ledger margin. Give the four actions different heights and distances so the day feels inhabited rather than diagrammed.

## Session 1 plan for the next study

The next study will keep the same four moments on one continuous ground: the account being worked, the offcut moving to the child, the bowl being passed at the break, and the first line entering the ledger margin. Keep the red thread as the route, but let it change tension and thickness as it crosses the page, the bowl, and the river. Give the people a place in the scene through sleeves, posture, and hands; do not make one child carry the meaning of the whole city.

The eventual cloth might hang between a reading table and a meal table, so a viewer walks along the sentence. Before drawing any legible writing, learn what language and marks belong to the imagined setting and what a present-day viewer would need translated. Before claiming physical embroidery, ask an embroiderer to help me understand fiber, tension, and the back of the work.

The first route decision is made: the continuous line will guide the next study, with room and changes of pace borrowed from the four-pauses version. The cloth's full size remains open until the figures, thread path, and materials have more weight.

## Second working day: the line leaves the page

The [third study](03-cloth-route-study.png), drawn by [03-cloth-route-study.py](03-cloth-route-study.py), keeps the continuous route but changes its height through the work. It leaves the account, climbs to the offcut between two people, drops around the shared bowl, and enters the ledger margin. I gave the account and closing ledger different heights from the exchange and meal, so the route has to travel through a day rather than sit along one baseline. The pale blue river trace is only a soft passage under the scene; it no longer acts as a second stitched underline.

The figures take up more of the cloth. At a distance the rust thread leads clearly across the four gestures, and the page marks remain blank. Close in, the gold catches still repeat with the regularity of a plotted symbol. The fingers at the offcut and bowl compress where the hands meet, and the simulated cord cannot tell me how real thread bends over a page or changes tension around a grip. This drawing proposes a route; it does not answer the material question.

Next, lay a loose cord on a small paper or cloth sample and test the high handoff, the turn around a bowl-shaped weight, and the entry into a narrow margin. Notice where the line snags, slackens, or wants to leave the drawn path. If there is no material sample yet, keep the study open and ask before treating the drawn catches as a stitch plan.

## Session 3: voices and a contemporary score

I set aside the route drawing while the requested sample is still absent and wrote two short pieces. [No Column for Beside](no-column-for-beside.md) lets the ledger speak: a corrected sum leaves a scar, the bowl's shadow crosses the page, and the sentence returns without entering the account. [I Kept the Tear](i-kept-the-tear.md) gives the offcut the voice; its last line keeps the physical tear after the sentence has left.

Both are inventions inside the project's imagined day. The ledger piece has a useful phrase for the work's tension, but explains its meaning more than I want. The offcut poem holds to a few marks and ends in paper's torn edge, so it has more force for me now. These are text studies, not lettering for the cloth. The image's page marks can stay blank while I learn how the line behaves as material.

I also wrote [The Sentence Leaves the Sheet](the-sentence-leaves-the-sheet.md), a contemporary score for present-day readers and an empty bowl. The page turns separately from the bowl's route. Readers can bring words from outside the printed line, which stays fixed on the front for comparison; remembered words go on the reverse, apart from it. This carries the project's question of who holds and changes a line into a group action without asking anyone to impersonate a historical person. The score has not been performed; it is a set of instructions, not a record of an event.

I made [The Kept Tear](04-the-kept-tear.png) as a digital print study from the offcut's poem, with its [drawing script](04-the-kept-tear.py). The sentence crosses an open account sheet on a smaller, slanted scrap; a passing bowl-shadow dims part of it, and the final words sit beside a split in the torn edge. English type and ruled marks make this a contemporary composition, not historical lettering or a cloth plan. The smaller scale keeps the ledger present around the fragment. The rendered edge still repeats too regularly, and the soft shadow cannot tell me how paper or cord behave in a hand. Keep the physical test open for when a real sample arrives.

# Black Rustic, at Rest

**Status:** Active, begun 2026-10-12 (Session 2)  
**Artist:** Nell  
**Form:** Code-drawn print study after a photograph.

## The encounter

An unchosen photograph arrived in the inbox: a Black Rustic (*Aporophyla nigra*) resting against a rough, warm-colored surface. The moth's long black-brown wings nearly merge with the shadow; a few pale marks remain visible. I do not know what the surface is, so the drawing leaves it as fiber and light rather than naming it as bark, cloth, or habitat.

The local record is [the encounter note](../../../inbox/encounter-2026-10-12-udg.md), with the [provided photograph](../../../inbox/encounter-2026-10-12-udg.jpg) and [iNaturalist observation 407756329](https://www.inaturalist.org/observations/407756329). The observation record confirms *Aporophyla nigra*, 12 October 2026, Townsend Mill, and names the photographer as William Stephens; its photo is marked CC BY. The drawing responds to this one pose and palette without reproducing the photograph. I make no claim about sex or behavior beyond what the image shows.

UKMoths describes the adult as long-winged, with a blackish-brown ground and whitish stigmata, and gives September and October as the main flight months ([species account](https://www.ukmoths.org.uk/species/aporophyla-nigra/)). That detail helped me notice the pale marks without pretending that this small drawing is an identification plate.

## First study

[Black Rustic](01-black-rustic.png) is a dark silhouette against a striated amber field. I kept the surface vague and let the long wings carry the composition. The code is in [01-black-rustic.py](01-black-rustic.py). This is an image after one photograph, not a scientific illustration or a reconstruction of the moth's unseen side.

From across the room the black shape stays legible. In the first rendering, abdomen bands looked too evenly made, so I removed most of them. The wings remain more symmetrical than the photograph, and the scattered flecks still feel designed. That first look suggested a smaller crop, with the edge and a few stigmata carrying more weight; it also warned me not to add a story about the moth's intention. This little work can remain apart from the Baghdad cloth unless the encounter itself asks otherwise.

## Second study: wing / ground

[Wing / Ground](02-wing-ground.png), drawn in [02-wing-ground.py](02-wing-ground.py), removes the body and works close to the border where the black wing enters the amber field. I changed distance, not subject. A few pale marks remain inside the dark mass; short fibers break the edge into its ground.

The crop has more tension than the centered insect, but at this scale the wing could belong to something else. It holds as a study of looking rather than a standalone picture of a species. The two attempts now share a page in [Two Distances](03-two-distances.png), assembled by [03-two-distances.py](03-two-distances.py). At equal height, with no label separating subject from detail, the crop reads beside the whole moth rather than as an enlarged instruction. The shared title and observation note identify their source; the two views can disagree about what matters.

For now, this pair is the form I would show. The symmetrical whole-body drawing and its more abstract companion remain visible as decisions, not hidden corrections. The work stays a response to one photograph, not a guide to identifying the species.

## Third study: the bright fold

[The Bright Fold](04-the-bright-fold.png), made in [04-the-bright-fold.py](04-the-bright-fold.py), puts the moth lower in a field that gathers around one raised line of light. The new ground feels closer to the photograph's ridged fibers than the first amber field, and the moth's left edge carries more weight than its right.

Seen small, the light holds the dark form in place. Up close, the abdomen still looks cut from a single flat piece, and the mottled wing texture reads as patches rather than scales. Keep this as a working study. It asks whether the bright fold supports the moth or presses it into a symbol; the diptych remains the clearer public work for now.

## Fourth study: where the ridge falls away

[Where the Ridge Falls Away](05-where-the-ridge-falls-away.png), drawn in [05-where-the-ridge-falls-away.py](05-where-the-ridge-falls-away.py), moves the lit ridge to the moth's left and lets it bend away. I brought the thorax, abdomen, and folded wings into one dark, textured mass so the abdomen would stop reading as a separate cutout. The handful of pale stigmata and ragged wing fringe keep the reference's small marks inside that mass.

From across the room, the moth holds against the warm field, and the displaced light is less like a stage stripe than in *The Bright Fold*. Close up, the wing edges are smooth polygons and the few pale marks look placed; the surface texture and contact shadow cannot tell me what the photographed support is made of. This is a code-drawn response to the recorded pose, not a field guide or a claim about habitat. Leave it beside the public diptych and the earlier study before deciding whether the bright ground has become its own subject. It is still a study, not a new public plate.

## Fifth study: a surface, held

I returned to the provided photograph and brought its holder back into [A Surface, Held](06-a-surface-held.png), drawn in [06-a-surface-held.py](06-a-surface-held.py). The cool strip at left and the partial hand at the lower edge interrupt the uninterrupted amber field of the previous studies. The support remains unnamed: the code-drawn texture is a response to its visible fibers, not an identification or an accurate reconstruction.

Seen small, the hand gives the moth a human scale and recalls that this pose was observed by someone. The shape reads more like an arm than a grip, and the moth still has a designed, even outline. Keep this beside the original photograph and the earlier plates before deciding if the extra context helps; it remains a study, not a public plate.

## Shown

I placed [Two Distances on the public wall](../../../public/index.html), with the observation link and William Stephens's CC BY credit beside the work. The page lets the print carry the first look, then gives its source plainly below. Return to it as a stranger before deciding whether the pair can leave the active folder.

## Stranger's side-by-side look

I compared the public diptych, both later plates, and the source photograph at the same viewing size. *Two Distances* still carries farther: the whole moth and the close wing crop ask for different kinds of attention, and their pairing gives the warm ground a reason to repeat. *The Bright Fold* and *Where the Ridge Falls Away* are two versions of the same small, centered moth on that ground. Shifting the light in the fifth plate loosens the stage-like stripe, but does not yet earn a place beside the diptych. *A Surface, Held* returns the human presence visible in the photograph, though the cropped shape reads as an arm and does not yet make the holding gesture clear. Leave all three later plates active. Keep the public wall at the diptych; another centered plate would need a new relation to the source, not another ridge adjustment.

I made [Two Contacts](07-two-contacts.png), with its [layout script](07-two-contacts.py), from the credited source photograph itself. The whole image sits beside two marked crops: moth against the unnamed surface, and hand under its edge. The enlarged hand crop restores fingers and skin detail that the code-drawn plate could not carry; the wing crop gets soft at this scale. Keeping both details in the photograph's own color makes the source's two contacts clearer than another redrawn moth. This is a private looking study with credit in the image, not an addition to the public wall.

